<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Declaration of Arbroath</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1320">1320</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Declaration of Arbroath is a letter dated 6 April 1320 from the barons and community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-xxii">Pope John XXII</a>, asking him to recognise Robert I as king and Scotland as independent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. It famously declares that the Scots would remove even Robert if he submitted to the English, and that they fought not for glory or riches but for freedom. It is the most celebrated document of Scottish history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/declaration-of-arbroath.jpg" alt="Declaration of Arbroath" width="320" height="337" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Scotland barons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Declaration_of_arbroath.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Declaration of Arbroath was Robert I's response to his excommunication for disobeying the pope's demand in 1317 for a truce in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-war-of-scottish-independence">First War of Scottish Independence</a>. The pope had recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-i-of-england">Edward I of England</a>'s claim to overlordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> in 1305, and had excommunicated Robert Bruce for the murder of John Comyn in Dumfries in 1306. That excommunication was lifted in 1308, and in 1320 John XXII excommunicated Robert again. The Declaration was part of a diplomatic campaign to assert Scotland's position as an independent kingdom and to lift the excommunication.</p><p>The wars began after the death of Alexander III in 1286 and of his heir, the Maid of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, in 1290. Robert was crowned at Scone in 1306, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bannockburn">Battle of Bannockburn</a> in 1314 secured his position. The letter, generally believed to have been written at Arbroath Abbey by Bernard of Kilwinning, then Chancellor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, was dated 6 April 1320.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The text describes the ancient history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scoti">Scoti</a>, who it claims came from Scythia Major through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>. It says the kingdom had known one hundred and thirteen kings of its own royal blood, and it compares Robert Bruce with Judah Maccabee and Joshua. It argues that Edward I unjustly attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, that Robert delivered the nation from this peril, and, most controversially, that the independence of Scotland was the prerogative of the Scottish people and not of the king. G. W. S. Barrow showed that a passage often quoted from James Fergusson's translation draws on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sallust">Sallust</a>'s Conspiracy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catiline">Catiline</a>.</p><p>The letter is the only survivor of three. The others came from Robert and from four Scottish bishops. Thirty-nine names stand at its start, and fifty-one <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> and nobles are said to have sealed it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The pope, influenced by an offer of Scottish support for his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>, urged Edward II to make peace, but he did not recognise Robert as king. On 1 March 1328 Edward III signed the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a> and Northampton, renouncing all English claims, and in October 1328 the pope lifted Robert's excommunication.</p><p>The Declaration was little known until the late 17th century, when its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> text was printed and translated into English, and it has since sometimes been described as a declaration of independence. Some read it as an early statement of popular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>; others argue that it was royal propaganda, justifying Bruce's rejection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-balliol">John Balliol</a>. The only surviving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> is held by the National Records of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Declaration of Arbroath” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Declaration of Arbroath</b> of 1320, a letter from the barons to Pope John XXII asserting the kingdom’s freedom and the right to replace a king who betrayed it, became one of the most quoted documents of Scottish history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Declaration of Arbroath</b> of 1320 announced that the Scottish nobles would choose another king if Robert the Bruce ever submitted to England.</blockquote><a href="/government/elective-monarchy">Elective monarchy · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
